  "synonym_discrimination": {
    "words": [
      "ill-adj",
      "sick-adj"
    ],
    "note": "**Sick** and **ill** both mean not in good health. **Sick** is more usual in American English and is the usual word before a noun (*a sick child*), although **ill** is used before a noun after an adverb (*seriously ill patients*). In British English, **ill** is usual after a verb (*she is ill*), and **be sick** often means *bring food up from the stomach*."
  },
